The proposed regulation includes the creation of a National Registration of PES and recommendations for tax incentives for participants. It also addresses socio-environmental safeguards to protect the rights of vulnerable communities and suggests governance structures for PES programs. Monitoring, verification, and credit rights for PES projects are discussed to ensure effectiveness and credibility, making PES a central tool for Brazil’s environmental and climate action strategies. The law and its regulation align with international commitments such as the UNFCCC COP 26, aiming to contribute to global environmental sustainability while fostering local economic development.', 'This document provides a technical proposal for the regulation of Law 14119/2021, which establishes the National Policy on Payment for Environmental Services (PES) in Brazil. The law aims to promote the conservation of ecosystems, water resources, soil, biodiversity, and related traditional knowledge, ensuring sustainable development in both rural and urban areas. The document emphasizes the role of various sectors—including governments, civil society, and the private sector—in supporting PES initiatives. It encourages the involvement of indigenous communities, family farmers, and traditional populations in PES, especially in private conservation reserves (RPPNs).

